>
> Wizards, and only wizards, can put Confuse Monsters on everything they
> whack for less than 2 spell points per victim.

Unless you know something I don't (entirely possible), they can't.  It
costs 5 per victim, same as any other class.  The Eye helps them regen
quickly, but it does the same  for anyone (and it's the primary wish
target for  most neutrals).


>  They can prepare this as
> much as 40 victims (not turns) in advance

Yes, but they screw themselves if they do so.  As I said, it's much
more likely that I want the opponents to behave deterministically than
randomly.  Stacking confuse monsters is a big loss, especially when
there are much better outs for dealing with the handful of really
dangerous opponents--having nearly everything behave randomly greatly
hurts your ability to control the situation, and increases your
chances of dying.

Or to put it another way, if confuse monster cost 0 spell points, I
still wouldn't stack it up on my primary weapon.  If I could stack it
on a backup, I might consider it.  But it's a horrible thing to have
stacked up in general.

> Wizards have so much magic resistance by the end of their quest that
> they probably should use a different cloak.  A robe perhaps?

Have you ever played nethack before?  Both Magicbane and your own
quest artifact are easily stealable, and wizards are excellent
casters--I could see preferring an elven cloak, protection, or oilskin
over MR, though I personally wouldn't.  But a robe?

Either you play moderately fast and loose--in which case the Eye and
magicbane suffice--or you are paranoid and wear the CoMR or GDSM
(unless you find another quest artifact).  But even  if you're fast
and loose, there's practically never a reason to wear a robe as a
standard item (rather than a once-in-a-while thing) for  wizards.

> That leaves two reasons to wield MB, to carry it around for curse
> protection against wandering monsters and to inflict afflictions instead
> of silver or other damage bonuses.

No.  Curse resistance is really the only reason to wield it.  Backuup
MR is nice but hardly necessary--it's  more important for that early
on for rogues et al who wish it up and can wield it to safely avoid
polytraps, dive for the castle w/o worrying as much about death rays,
etc.  Really, 1-turn E is the biggest effect early on, but that
doesn't require wielding it.  Later on, curse resistance is huge.



